T02.significance:
Borderline for a main track. A NeurIPS/ICLR reviewer will likely read it as PermuQuant's sorting lemma moved from the channel axis to the token axis, plus Xi et al.'s leverage score, applied to a niche setting (FP4 backward in RL, which few systems run today, though TRIAGE and HiFloat4 show it exists). It becomes publishable only with three things:
- a genuinely new theorem: a sort-versus-RHT crossover in the tail index, or a bound that handles both operands of the shared permutation;
- measured heavy tails of inner-layer token gradients in RL;
- a clear end-to-end FP4-RL gain over the standard RHT+SR recipe at negligible kernel cost.

Otherwise it is a workshop or systems-track contribution.

T10.what_remains:
I found no paper that does any of the following:
1. computes element-wise SR variance in the FP4/NVFP4 wgrad, including in the RHT-rotated basis, and feeds it into the optimizer;
2. measures the ratio sigma_q^2/v per tensor in a real NVFP4 recipe;
3. tests whether heterogeneity of that ratio across tensors explains the FP4-vs-BF16 gap or a shift in the optimal LR, beyond what a scalar or per-tensor LR multiplier fixes.

What is left is an empirical diagnostic plus a modest element-wise extension of DP-AdamBC.
